Item 03 is complete. Nothing further is needed: the report section, code, checks, PROGRESS entry and iteration mark are written, the gate passes, and the driver does the commit.

**What this iteration produced**

The reduction catalogue is `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §3, with sixteen rows: every reduction into pathwidth or vertex separation found in the held papers, each with source problem, construction, target vertex count, exact relation and the source problem's standing after ETH. The classical NP-completeness reductions are all superlinear by the same mechanism, since each uses clique size to simulate a weight or an offset. Lengauer's is quadratic, Gustedt's quadratic on 3-regular graphs, Megiddo et al.'s cubic, and Mihai and Todinca's pseudo-polynomial. Three papers that might carry a linear chain from a hypothesis (Bonnet, Arnborg–Corneil–Proskurowski, Monien–Sudborough) are not held and are marked unknown rather than superlinear. Kashiwabara and Fujisawa 1979 is recorded as missing.

**Three linear constructions, ours, proved and checked.** A closed-neighbourhood cobipartite double with `vs(H) = vs(G) + n`, so pathwidth on cobipartite graphs is exactly as hard as in general at blow-up 2. A clique blow-up multiplying `pw + 1` by `c`. A degree-clique expansion with `cw(G) = vs(G°)` for maximum degree 3, planarity preserved, at most `3n` vertices. All were found by brute force on graphs to 6 vertices before being stated, then proved, then checked on all 1,252 atlas graphs to 7 vertices with zero failures (`learning/subexp_reductions.py`, log under the scratch directory). A refuter subagent read the six proof directions blind and found all sound, with two editorial points folded in.

**The best linear X** is cutwidth on subcubic graphs. A `2^{o(n)}` pathwidth algorithm would settle Bentert et al.'s explicit open problem for cutwidth on that class. No linear reduction from a problem with a surviving post-ETH lower bound is on record, so item 07's theorem transfers to cutwidth and to cobipartite pathwidth, not to a hypothesis.

**Gate.** The first run timed out in a pathwidth_solver test under a machine load of 36 to 51 (the owner's other work, with the gate at nice 19). That test passes in half a second alone and the second full gate run passed cleanly. Nothing under `pathwidth_solver/` was touched; the first log is kept in case it recurs.

**Next** is items 04 to 06 in any order. Item 04 should start from the finding that no class containing the cobipartite graphs can be an easier class for the positive track.

